<article class="env-proposition" id="A272F0">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/A272F0">Proposition <span data-tag="A272F0">5.4.7.10</span>.</a> Let $\calC $ be an accessible category, and let $\{  \calD _{\alpha } \subseteq \calC \} _{\alpha \in A}$ be a (small) collection of accessible subcategories of $\calC $. Then $\bigcap _{\alpha \in A} \calD _{\alpha }$ is an accessible subcategory of $\calC $. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We have a homotopy Cartesian diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { \bigcap _{\alpha \in A} \calD _{\alpha } \ar [r]^-{i'} \ar [d] &amp;  \calC \ar [d]^{f} \\ \prod _{\alpha \in A} \calD _{\alpha } \ar [r]^-{i} &amp;  \calC ^{A}} \]
</div>
    <p>
       Lemma <a href="/tag/98D8C5" data-tag="98D8C5">5.4.7.2</a> implies that $\prod _{\alpha \in A} \calD _{\alpha }$ and $\calC ^{A}$ are accessible, and it is easy to see that $f$ and $i$ are accessible functors. Applying Proposition <a href="/tag/950E9C" data-tag="950E9C">5.4.6.6</a>, we conclude that $\bigcap _{\alpha \in A} \calD _{\alpha }$ is accessible, and that $i'$ is an accessible functor, as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>